> E.g. "I like the bookage",
> as distinct from "I like all of the bookage" {mi nelci (piro) lei
> cukta},

And how would those differ in meaning? You either like it or you
don't. If you want to add whether there are components that you like
or not, you may do so. To like something, do you have to like
each component, or most components, or just the general effect
they produce? That's a matter of the meaning of "like", independent
of masses.

>"I like some (and possibly all) of the bookage" {mi nelci
> pisuo lei cukta}.

Right.

> Just as {mi nelci le pa cukta} means just "I like
> the book" rather than "I like all of the book"

I don't see much difference, other than pragmatics. Unless
by "all of the book" you mean "each part of the book", but that
is not what {piro} gives you, it gives you the book as a whole,
not each one of its parts by itself.

It's like saying that there is a difference between "the set
of all books" and "the whole set of all books". They are the
same thing. {piro} is needed only in contrast with the
possible {pisu'o}, it doesn't have any more significance than
that.

